<Project>
  <!--
    GL-27: consumers pin exact versions of our own local packages, so adopting a new contract
    version is a visible edit rather than something NuGet decides for you.

    `Version="0.1.0"` on a PackageReference is a FLOOR ("0.1.0 or newer"), not a pin — NuGet is
    free to float to any newer version it finds acceptable. `Version="[0.1.0]"` (the bracketed
    range form) is the only syntax that means exactly 0.1.0 and nothing else. This target makes
    the difference a build error instead of a convention nobody checks: every PackageReference
    naming one of our own package ids (BuildingBlocks, BuildingBlocks.*, GiftLists.Contracts,
    Identity.Contracts, Reservations.Contracts — the same ids nuget.config maps exclusively to
    ../local-feed, see that file) must use the exact bracketed form.

    WHY THIS IS Directory.Build.targets AND NOT Directory.Build.props. It belongs beside
    Directory.Build.props conceptually — same synced-root-file mechanism, same "every project
    inherits it" reasoning — but not textually in that file: TargetFrameworkTests in every
    service's Architecture/ suite asserts Directory.Build.props is byte-identical, verbatim, to
    the exact block CONVENTIONS.md "Target framework" documents, and CONVENTIONS.md is out of
    this change's scope to edit. Directory.Build.targets is auto-imported by the SDK for every
    project exactly like Directory.Build.props is (after the project body instead of before), is
    not pinned against any documented verbatim block, and is added to the same
    scripts/sync-repo-roots.sh synced-file set and repo-root-files.sha256 manifest as
    Directory.Build.props and nuget.config, so it drifts exactly as loudly as they do.

    WHY A BUILD-TIME TARGET AND NOT AN ARCHITECTURE TEST. It has to catch every consumer csproj
    in every one of the five repos, including test projects that reference BuildingBlocks.Testing
    — not just one service's own production code, which is the boundary
    Architecture/Support/SourceFiles.cs draws for that service's own suite. An architecture test
    also cannot see another repo's csproj at all: it runs inside one repo's own test assembly.
    Duplicating a text-based check five times over, one per repo, would be exactly the kind of
    per-repo drift that this file's own sync mechanism exists to prevent, and it would fail late
    — only when that repo's own unit test suite happens to run — where a build-time target fails
    at the first `dotnet build`, before a single line of C# compiles.

    Deliberately NOT enforced on `dotnet restore` alone — restore does not run the Build target
    graph, so a bare restore of a bad pin succeeds (verified empirically). `dotnet build` and
    `dotnet test` both invoke restore first, so in every workflow that matters the bad pin still
    fails loudly, just at the build step rather than the restore step.
  -->
  <Target Name="EnforceExactLocalPackagePins" BeforeTargets="Build;Pack;VSTest">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_LocalPackageReference Include="@(PackageReference)" Condition="
        '%(Identity)' == 'BuildingBlocks' Or
        $([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::IsMatch('%(Identity)', '^BuildingBlocks\..+$')) Or
        '%(Identity)' == 'GiftLists.Contracts' Or
        '%(Identity)' == 'Identity.Contracts' Or
        '%(Identity)' == 'Reservations.Contracts'
      " />
      <_NonExactLocalPackageReference Include="@(_LocalPackageReference)" Condition="
        !$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::IsMatch('%(_LocalPackageReference.Version)', '^\[[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+(-[0-9A-Za-z\.\-]+)?\]$'))
      " />
    </ItemGroup>
    <Error
      Condition="'@(_NonExactLocalPackageReference)' != ''"
      Text="GL-27: local GiftList packages must be pinned to an exact version, e.g. Version=&quot;[0.1.0]&quot; -- a bare Version=&quot;0.1.0&quot; is a floor, not a pin (ARCHITECTURE.md &quot;What &#39;breaking&#39; means for a message contract&quot;). Found %(_NonExactLocalPackageReference.Identity) Version=&quot;%(_NonExactLocalPackageReference.Version)&quot; in $(MSBuildProjectFullPath)." />
  </Target>
</Project>
